WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Hey good humans! Before we get into this episode of Middle Ground, here's a quick teaser of 'The Leap' that's coming soon.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.520
Very shaky

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.420
I'm about to call my dad.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.660
My ex-boyfriend.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.500
Did you love me?

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.540
You're the one who told me face-to-face that I am no longer your daughter

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:19.640
Wish me luck.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.820
So stay on the lookout for that video of 'The Leap' that's coming out soon

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.420
...and if you want to challenge yourself and reach out to someone to reconnect and take the leap,

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.660
then click on the link in the description below.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.960
And now enjoy this episode of Middle Ground.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.540
There are people making the argument that we should end self isolation

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.880
because a few hundred thousand deaths is worth sacrificing

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.239
for the economy, some people would agree with that

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:48.760
until it's their mother who dies, or their uncle, or their grandfather.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.360
Nearly a dozen other states will start to ease their

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.960
restrictions as they slowly start the reopening process...

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.481
...May not come back at all.

00:00:57.481 --> 00:00:59.579
There will be coronavirus in the fall.

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:04.400
I can see the emotional agony it causes in patients and their family members.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.390
Restrictions should have been put on the people that are sick!

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.900
I don't think it's a good thing for our economy or our country.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.830
So I had to layoff my employees. We have no work

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.340
I believe that it violates our most basic of civil rights.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:22.160
I wouldn't say that I'm necessarily anti-self isolation, but more so anti-government imposed self isolation.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.040
Our new normal does not mean that we will sacrifice our freedoms for our safety.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.420
The key thing is is that working with good data is going to allow the US government to continue to make better decisions.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:37.320
I'm not able to work in my given profession, despite this, I am pro-quarantine.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.380
I own my own practice, but because of the quarantine, I've only seen patients two times a week.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:49.500
It might be hard to understand why people would gather like this while a deadly virus ravages America.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.000
What if it was your mother?

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.700
What if your infant son or daughter gets sick?

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.460
My name is Cedric Jamie Rutland, I'm a pulmonary critical care physician

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:19.460
...in Southern California, Newport Beach and Riverside, California,

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.060
And I am pro self-isolation.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.640
Hi, I'm Dagny. I'm an ophthalmologist,

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.520
I live here with my husband I'm an expecting mom to be here in Orange County, California,

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.020
and I am pro self-isolation.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.040
Hi, I'm Alex. I live with my boyfriend and another roommate in a very small apartment and,

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.300
I'm pro self-isolation.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.880
My name is Austin. I'm a chiropractor.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.980
I live here in Newport Beach, California.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.380
I would consider myself anti self-isolation.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:54.540
Hi, I'm Ali-Marie. I am a massage and craniosacral therapist by trade, but I'm currently a stay-at-home mom

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.860
And I'm about 20 weeks pregnant, and

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.800
I am anti self-isolation.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.880
Hi, I am Julia. I live with my family here in Texas.

00:03:03.887 --> 00:03:05.900
I am anti self-isolation.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.400
I know somebody who has been infected with COVID 19.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.920
Can the disagrees, please, turn off their cameras?

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:25.180
Dr. Dagny: I know a physician colleague in New York who was infected and actually is an expecting mom,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.840
but thankfully she's doing okay and on the road to recovery.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.620
I also know someone from my neighborhood,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:36.220
who unfortunately passed away, and he's actually relatively young.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.780
Dr. Credric: One old partner of mine who's little younger than me,

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:43.820
...probably 34 or 35 who was infected with COVID-19. His case was mild.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:49.200
I had another physician friend of mine, who was older, probably about 55 or so.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.000
He was infected with COVID and I had to take care of him.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.580
Dr. Austin: I'll talk to a little bit different point maybe.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:58.060
One of my co-workers here, her father had a heart attack,

00:03:58.079 --> 00:04:01.720
...but he was diagnosed with COVID at the hospital.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:05.420
And ultimately, they were told that COVID-19 killed the patient.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:11.100
When really it seems that the heart attack and complications from cardiovascular disease were ultimately the issue.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.900
The other health care professionals in the group here,

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:18.540
I would love to hear your opinion on that as far as what you maybe heard about how they're counting.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.240
Who qualifies as a COVID case and what the cause of death was necessarily?

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.740
Dr. Cedric: Here's what we've discovered:

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:30.480
When you look at these patients that have COVID-19 is as a result of the presence of the virus

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:36.960
...within your blood vessels and your blood stream, it leads to the activation of the coagulation system,

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.040
so their blood can clot easier.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.960
So we've seen heart attacks.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.520
We've seen strokes.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.539
We've seen pulmonary embolisms

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.380
And so your point is well-taken.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:52.760
He died of a heart attack. Yes, but COVID-19 is a significant risk factor of coagulation.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:55.380
The disagreeres can join the conversation now.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.140
Ali-Marie: I do want to talk about the antibody test that they've started,

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:05.020
...and the extreme percentages that they have seen.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.300
There was the one that they did randomized in California recently,

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.060
where they were getting as high as 55%.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.900
For all I know I've had it.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:17.140
My daughter and I were sick for four weeks, six weeks this winter.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:23.159
Right now in Michigan, Governor Whitmer is calling for all essential workers to be tested,

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.420
...but there's no antibody tests to go right alongside it.

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:30.660
So my husband, who is an essential worker, could go get tested comes back negative.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.540
What? Does he have to get a weekly test now?

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:38.160
When he could have already had it this winter with us and just had very mild symptoms?

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.099
Alex: I'd like to make a point because I want the doctors in the group chat to respond to this.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.880
I have heard that getting COVID does not mean you won't get it again.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.600
Dr. Dagny: There have definitely been cases in the US and outside

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.520
showing that those who recovered actually went on and tested positive again for the virus,

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.360
so that is a little bit concerning

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.140
because we were all kind of betting that once you get infected, you'd be immune.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:04.960
That previous assumption was based on what we know from some older viruses from before.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:08.240
It just goes to show that we still don't know a lot about this virus.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:13.120
Question: "The scare of the virus is over-hyped."

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:24.720
Julia: I think that the scare has been largely driven by the media.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.200
I know that initially, there were models that came out of

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.600
the Imperial College of London that claimed that millions of people would die.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.980
That was really used as the justification for these massive lock downs,

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:40.560
and we have not seen those numbers pan out at all.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.100
Dr. Austin: I totally agree with you that a lot of what we're seeing is media created hysteria.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.060
There's a lot of new information that's coming out,

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.000
we really think people were trying to do their best with the data that they were provided at the time.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.339
I especially have an issue with how the data is actually being collected and then ultimately how the deaths are being counted.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:05.480
Dr. Cedric: Since we initiated social distancing, especially when you look at, California,

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:08.820
you can see that the amount of infections has slowed,

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:10.720
and I actually saw this in the hospital.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.560
I saw the uptake and then all of a sudden as the governor got more restriced,

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:17.080
and as the state got more restricted in terms of isolation,

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:22.020
You can see that California was beginning to flatten the curve and actually lead the nation in that.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.620
I agree with the self-isolation principle

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.700
because it did what it was supposed to do:

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:32.060
which was prevent the amount of infections to bombard the hospital

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.740
because that's not what you want.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:35.700
I mean, we still have physicians getting infected,

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.520
and the more infections you have in the hospital,

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.540
the more likely it is for one of us to get infected

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:43.920
and then you have a reduction of healthcare workers to be able to manage these patients.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.740
Dr. Dagny: Someone called it even well ahead of time.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.360
If we find that weeks or months from now,

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:55.300
the cases are not as bad as projected, people will say that we overreacted, when the truth is,

00:07:55.300 --> 00:08:00.340
that's just evidence that the things that we're doing right now, including self-isolation has worked.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:06.740
For those people who continue to compare, you know, COVID-19 to a really bad flu season,

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:07.840
well, let's look at the data.

00:08:07.849 --> 00:08:12.820
They're saying that okay - a bad flu season we have 60,000 deaths in the U.S.

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:15.900
Right now with COVID-19, We have about 50,000.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.060
So it's comparable.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.680
Well, we have to look at the rate at which that's happening.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:21.520
That's in one year.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:26.200
Right now we're in April and we're already matching the worst flu season.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:29.000
I do agree the media is always biased, but when

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.860
we have epidemiological and infectious disease experts calling

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.020
this a pandemic, calling this as something unprecedented,

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:38.920
I think that's real and that is not hype.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:42.900
Julia: In terms of the hospitals being filled to capacity,

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:46.240
that's obviously a situation that nobody would want.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.760
It appears to me that the US healthcare system

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:57.320
Didn't become as swamped as they originally predicted, and I think that going back in history

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:02.900
quarantines tend to be for the sick, as opposed to the healthy, and I wonder if maybe

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:09.420
we would take more of an approach where the sick are being quarantined, which I think is is very

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:15.180
justifiable as opposed to everybody who's healthy being quarantined, that might be more appropriate.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:21.140
Dr. Cedric: The issue with that is that we don't know who's sick with SARS-Cov-2.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:25.960
There are many different estimations of how many people are asymptomatic.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:31.800
I myself, have seen people be asymptomatic that are positive for the virus, so that becomes an issue.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:35.720
At least with the Spanish flu, you knew who was sick.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:41.600
And so until testing is rampant throughout the country, and we're able to know who is asymptomatic,

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.000
I agree with you - quarantining the people who have SARS-Cov-2.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:51.120
If we tested everyone, is the most appropriate and probably the single best treatment

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.840
that we can provide across the nation.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:10:00.860
Ali-Marie: With the United States, our judicial system is founded and rooted within innocent until proven guilty.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:04.240
Now, I'm not saying there's anything wrong with us acting like we're a carrier,

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:09.080
washing our hands using antibacterial sanitizer.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:15.260
But ultimately, I don't think it's right or fair for the government to say you're not showing symptoms,

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:17.620
but you could be sick, so you're gonna stay home

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:21.460
It's like sending somebody on trial to prison because they could have been guilty.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:24.650
Alex: This isn't like putting people in prison because they're sick.

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:29.150
I think this is actually more of a political difference than like I guess a medical argument.

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:32.220
There are people making the argument that we should end self isolation because

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:36.880
a few hundred thousand deaths is worth sacrificing for the economy.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:38.360
I disagree with that.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:40.080
Some people would agree with that until

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:41.360
it's their mother who dies,

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:42.300
or their uncle,

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:43.400
or their grandfather.

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:50.480
Question: "Slowing the curve is more important than human rights during the quarantine."

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.679
Alex: Alright, guess I'll be the devil's advocate and just

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:02.860
I'm going to take, I'm gonna take issue with the framing.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:05.550
Probably a better phrase would be civil rights perhaps.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:09.920
Limiting people's ability to gather is certainly an infringement on civil rights,

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:12.299
but I would agree that the actions that have been

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:14.820
taken are worth slowing the curve.

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:16.080
Everyone can attack me now.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.880
Dr. Austin: There's no attacking, man. It's a hard one.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:24.360
Dr. Cedric: There's no attacking, I just wasn't gonna make a ridiculous like extreme statement. That's crazy.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.040
Ali-Marie: So I'm in Michigan

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.520
where some of the most extreme

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:35.849
lock downs have taken place. Governor Whitmer just today extended our stay at home order 15 days, but

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:39.500
Relaxed some of the restrictions that everyone was so mad about.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:43.289
As far as you know, there was a huge Lansing protest recently

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:48.150
Operation Gridlock that was organized by the Michigan Conservative Coalition.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:51.180
That was all about the fact that she was

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:57.040
Not allowing all of these businesses to work that were able to practice social distancing

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.540
some governors are willing to go to whatever extent it takes

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:05.320
because it definitely has been an infringement on

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:11.640
like all of our civil rights to be able to go out and in Harris County, I believe it's in Texas, right?

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:14.009
That just said that you'll be fined $1,000

00:12:14.009 --> 00:12:16.900
If you don't wear a mask in public. 
Julia: The local police

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:21.000
Union in Harris County actually came out and said they will not be enforcing that

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:22.820
because they view it as unconstitutional.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:24.940
There's definitely a balance there between

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:27.600
federal overreach versus states rights.

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:33.040
So California even, where you are, it's a very different scenario

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:35.000
in terms of the level of outbreak,

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:36.300
the number of patients.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:40.100
Taking kind of an individual approach I think is important.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:43.840
If you're in a state that has seen very few cases,

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:46.440
I don't know that the response needs to be the same.

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:48.600
The federal government stepping in would make it a

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:51.820
One-size-fits-all, and I don't know that any of us

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:53.730
...would feel that was appropriate.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:13:00.120
Alex: I think that charging people a $1000 fine for not having face mask is classist and is regressive.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:02.700
Who's less likely to have access to masks?

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:05.640
It's poor people, and they don't have a thousand dollars to pay a fine.

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:08.420
I agree that you know, you have to find a fine line

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:12.989
between an authoritarian response and something that's responsible to all people.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:17.236
I think that the federal government and I'm talking about both parties here.

00:13:17.236 --> 00:13:19.409
I have a lot of issues with both sides.

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:21.855
I think they need to be taking a leading role.

00:13:21.855 --> 00:13:24.460
When you have disparate responses from states,

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:31.120
what you risk is states with a less extreme reaction or protocol.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:35.220
You risk those people going across state borders, and actually spreading the virus more.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:39.920
And we don't want to get to a point where we have to shut down state borders.

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:47.220
Question: The US government is handling the crisis well.

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:56.800
Ali-Marie: I am very torn on how I actually feel about this.

00:13:56.900 --> 00:13:58.700
My dad owns a small business,

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:04.020
I've read a lot of the different arguments on the Payroll Protection Plan

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:06.860
and a bunch of the different deals with Shake Shack,

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:11.780
and everything else which... there are a lot of details that go into that.

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:18.620
75% of any loan that's given to the business owners has to be paid in to employee benefits.

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:20.620
He has a company has 220 employees.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:24.800
So you're talking $65 million in revenue every year,

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.580
and so some would say his business is too big, he shouldn't have gotten it, but that's

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:31.760
220 employees that he's taken care of.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:33.680
There can be an emotional response, saying

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:37.760
"They're not protecting the mom-and-pop shops. They're not protecting this...", but ultimately

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:40.580
220 employees are getting their salaries,

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:41.720
and 4 aren't.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:48.220
And while it's emotionally sad to see those smaller businesses forced to close,

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:52.300
we're gonna be in a little bit of a depression, we're gonna hit a recession.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:54.220
And so we're all in this together.

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:58.480
And I think the bipartisan support has been really

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:04.480
lacking especially in the beginning, but I think that they're somehow finding a way.

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:10.800
Dr. Cedric: So all I'm saying, and I think it's really simple

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.120
is you have all this money that we're giving to businesses,

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:14.900
and I think it's appropriate and I think that they should.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:16.320
The issue is just testing.

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:19.500
Take some of that money, and give the country some tests.

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:23.120
Antibody testing and the PCR testing.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:28.880
So until that's done and distributed across the country, no, I don't think it's a job well done.

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:31.020
Julia: Can you tell me why that's such a priority?

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:33.180
I know we need to know how many people have it.

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:35.369
But can you explain to me why that's so critical?

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:36.700
I just don't know.

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:39.700
Dr. Cedric: If we give everyone an antibody test,

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.780
We're gonna know who the sick are.

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:44.440
IGM is what gets produced acutely

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:48.689
It's a type of antibody in your bloodstream - SARS COV 2, so you'll know who's infected.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:52.600
IgG means that you've had a sustained response to the system

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:56.040
and your body has had enough time to develop a neutralizing antibody.

00:15:56.050 --> 00:16:00.400
So you'll be able to quarantine the "sick" without being symptomatic.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:02.699
Alex: I haven't gotten my stimulus check yet.

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:04.140
I don't know about anybody else.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:06.900
My rent has already been due, where the hell is the check?

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:08.438
I already need another check.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:13.980
The government to be focusing on helping individuals who are stuck at home and who have no way of surviving.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:17.300
I think the fact that the federal government, the White House specifically,

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:21.200
for months, was saying that this was a hoax and that this was completely blown out of

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.200
proportion, was hugely detrimental to our response.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:27.578
Dr. Dagny: South Korea did a great job, Taiwan did a great job,

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:30.900
but unfortunately, that is not what I can say for the United States.

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:35.300
Things are turning around and all these stimulus packages that they're doing, the fact

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:39.620
that they're vowing to expand testing is definitely on a better track,

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:43.200
and my husband he was one of the first in line when that CARES package came out

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:47.700
to sign up for the forgivable business loan and the PPP.

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:52.120
He didn't get it, and it was heartbreaking to hear that

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:55.080
these large corporations were getting access to that money.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:58.780
So even though the government was doing something of good intent,

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:02.500
the way that it was executed unfortunately wasn't the best.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:06.660
Dr. Austin: Dagny you mentioned that South Korea, that you admired South Korea, how their response was.

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:11.140
Well to my knowledge, they had trackers on everybody's cellular device

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:13.000
that was monitoring where they were going.

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:19.080
That just in itself to me is a completely asinine principle from the First Amendment,

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:23.600
like the fact that government is tracking your every move seems a lot like house arrest.

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:28.300
Dr. Dagny: The tracker system that South Korea used has generally been praised by most countries,

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:30.700
and one of the main reasons that they were avoid to -

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:34.400
able to avoid a peak and have to implement quarantine.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:39.390
And we the U.S, are actually taking lessons from them, but it's not as authoritarian as you think.

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:43.540
We all have the option to opt out, but the only way this is going to work is if you opt in.

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:49.920
And that's for people who decide that the public good is of higher benefit right now,

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:52.960
than our own individual, I guess, concerns for privacy.

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:56.500
Ali-Marie: So Dagny you're talking about different countries and what they're seeing.

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:00.200
Sweden never did a lock down.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:04.020
They said if you're vulnerable and at-risk stay home, don't go to work.

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:09.160
And their chief Epidemiologist right now, is saying that they are very close to reaching herd immunity.

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:15.880
So, within this whole idea of staying home to avoid a second wave, a second wave is inevitable.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:19.220
Right now, the federal government recommendation on

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:20.560
when you can reopen your economy

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:24.360
is if you see a daily decline in positive cases over two weeks and

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:28.180
regardless of how when those two weeks are it's gonna start to rise again

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:30.220
when everyone starts being around each other.

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:35.980
There is no negating it we can't stay locked up in our houses for three, four, five, six months,

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:41.100
there's some doctors saying that we're gonna still be seeing COVID cases for 18 months.

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:43.220
Dr. Dagny: When we compared the U.S to other countries,

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:47.660
I think we were more similar to the tracks of Italy and China.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.040
I don't think herd immunity works for every country.

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:54.720
It's really gonna be specific to you know, the region itself.

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:59.820
Ali-Marie: I'm comparing Michigan to Sweden because they both have approximately 10 million people

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:03.020
and their population, population density is about the same and

00:19:03.020 --> 00:19:05.020
Sweden has a far less death rate

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:08.360
-- and infection rate than Michigan does
Dr. Dagny: --But again, Michigan is very heterogeneous compared to Sweden.

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:10.920
Alex: Dagney, I feel like we have like the same mind or something because

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:12.820
I was just I brought up this article about Sweden,

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:15.240
and the situation there is very different.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:20.600
They have far more single person households than the United States does.

00:19:20.618 --> 00:19:23.540
They have a much more robust social safety net,

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:28.980
so people who want to opt out of exposing themselves don't have to worry about not paying their bills.

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:33.740
And the Swedish government is actually reconsidering their herd immunity approach,

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:35.780
and considering taking stricter measures

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:38.420
because they're seeing the cases in Stockholm rise.

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:43.300
There are people in the Swedish government who are saying they might regret the herd immunity approach,

00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:45.820
and it is a much more homogeneous society.

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:47.000
You know, I hate to say it,

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:53.380
but there are societies that take more responsibility to the health of like everyone around them.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:56.020
America is a very individualist country.

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.280
Question: "The lock down is harming my mental health."

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:10.440
Dr. Austin: I have patients that are still coming in so we get asked about this every day like,

00:20:10.460 --> 00:20:12.240
"Dr. Quan, what do you think about the lockdown?"

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:13.260
"What do you think's going on?"

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:17.310
I've not only been doing and working my normal scheduled hours here at the office.

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:22.000
But then we go home and I'll spend hours and hours and hours until the wee hours of the morning,

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:25.640
researching trying to stay up on the new stuff that's that's coming out on the corona virus.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:28.240
So it has been very fatiguing and toiling on me

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:32.080
just being able to take care of patients and do my normal daily routine.

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:38.240
Obviously no deaths are discounted or lost or you know, we care about everybody who's dying in all this

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:41.480
But you got to look at like child molestation rates that are going up,

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:46.180
you're looking at spousal abuse rates going up, suicidal issues, depression, anxiety.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:50.980
Overall lifestyle choices people are making and especially mental health concerns through this

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:54.060
isolation is definitely another factor that we're looking at.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:57.320
We might be stealing from Peter to pay Paul, this kind of idea

00:20:57.320 --> 00:21:00.400
when it comes to you know, we're we're looking at the deaths actually occurring.

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:05.560
Julia: Has my mental health been negatively affected?

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:10.300
I feel like that would maybe be stating it a little bit too strongly some of you have mentioned

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:14.800
We we appear to be going the route of a depression. That's a scary thing.

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:15.750
It's really sad.

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:18.280
It's gonna have a profound impact on people's lives,

00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:21.900
and this might be too personal, but I feel like ultimately God's in control,

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:25.360
and I have to just you know, that's something that I have to accept

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:27.660
and and it gives me a little bit of peace, too.

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:31.920
Dr. Dagny: I feel like not having a lock down would probably affect me more.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:34.980
Because it's the infection that scares me more.

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:40.480
If I had to continue my normal patient caseload and surgery caseload at my practice,

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:43.300
and be paranoid about every patient thinking,

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:44.160
"Are they infected?"

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:45.850
"Are they going to infect me?"

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:48.720
And the fact that our supplies our PPE is running down,

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:50.250
as well in my own private practice,

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:53.280
that would be more mentally straining to me personally.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:59.420
Dr. Cedric: It's not that the presence of all of these restrictions is not harming my mental health.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:04.320
It's just that I feel that the restrictions are appropriate.

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:10.120
My job as a pulmonary and critical care physician has changed forever.

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:16.060
I have to now worry about COVID-19 in almost every patient that I see

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:19.680
because I'm a pulmonologist, and they present with respiratory failure.

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:26.240
And I have to be super protective and super cautious when I'm dealing with an outpatient,

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:28.460
when I'm dealing with an inpatient,

00:22:28.460 --> 00:22:33.500
on-call in the ICU as I was last night from here on out.

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:36.380
Until the vaccine comes or whatever,

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:39.420
and this is something I have to deal with because I have two small little kids,

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:43.020
I have a five-year-old and a seven-year-old little girl, and I got my wife and

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:46.100
I just feel that I have to protect them.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:50.580
Question: I have felt judged by the other side

00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:59.940
Julia: If you oppose continued quarantine mass quarantine, I should say,

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:05.520
people judge you and make the assumption that you don't care about somebody's life.

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:07.620
That's categorically not true.

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:12.960
I care very much for people that are suffering and people that are dying, and I don't want

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:14.900
for that to happen to anybody.

00:23:14.900 --> 00:23:19.900
Ali-Marie: My sister-in-law, specifically, she was not happy

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:24.680
because I had said that Whitmer was thinking about extending the order 70 days,

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:27.320
and how I wasn't sure I felt about it, and I mean

00:23:27.320 --> 00:23:30.020
We're not even actually on speaking terms right now

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:34.840
because she was so angry at me that I would even potentially think

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:38.620
That we shouldn't all be quarantined by the government right now.

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:40.620
Dr. Austin: As a doctor, like I truly believe

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:44.820
I took an oath to protect my community and to always have my patients' best

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:50.060
interest in mind and to really always be doing new innovative research to look for new ways to help take care of people.

00:23:50.069 --> 00:23:53.920
This is just something where now that I've seen what I've seen I've looked at the data.

00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:56.580
I've done my own analysis and research on it.

00:23:56.580 --> 00:23:58.980
I've talked to some of the leading experts in the field at this point.

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:03.620
And there's enough evidence for me to go out and protest for people

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:06.240
who may not know the data and may not really understand

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:08.260
some of that kind of stuff that's going on.

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:11.220
Dr. Cedric: This is a very interesting question, I'm sure you guys are surprised that I'm here,

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:17.100
so most of my friends are in the financial industry, and so their

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:18.700
respective fields are suffering.

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:23.580
From them, what they say to me most often is well, you're a physician

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:29.440
And your business hasn't been affected, and not only that but for the first time in my career

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:32.720
Instead of my presence being one of relief,

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:35.220
it's now one of fear.

00:24:35.230 --> 00:24:39.820
The first thing a non-COVID patient asked me is were you in a COVID room earlier today.

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:43.620
Every time I make a right or left I'm being judged.

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:50.000
Dr. Dagny: Pretty lucky to say that I haven't really been judged based on my position

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:53.660
because I think I'm actually in the majority group in

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:56.060
advocating for self isolation.

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:59.420
I mean I've been judged on plenty of other things being on social media,

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:01.860
one of the things I recently spoke up for was

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:06.640
you know to end violence and hate against Asian American people of descent because of this pandemic.

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:09.660
And I think ultimately we're all on the same page,

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:13.020
we all want what's better for the greater good,

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:15.500
and you know to you guys you think that is

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:17.820
getting the economy back up again.

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:23.440
If we hit it hard, like we are right now with the quarantine and we continue to stay on track,

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:27.620
I think we all ultimately get to go back to a normal life faster.

00:25:27.650 --> 00:25:30.862
I don't think Pro-self isolation means that we're anti-economy.

00:25:30.862 --> 00:25:33.300
I'm with you guys. I want the economy to open back.

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:38.569
But I only want it to happen when it's safe to do so and that's just based on, you know, objective data.

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.560
Question: "I Have learned something from the other side."

00:25:47.540 --> 00:25:51.780
Ali-Marie: Can I ask a question? Do you mean in this conversation or like in general?

00:25:51.780 --> 00:25:52.900
Host: In this conversation.

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:54.780
Ali-Marie: Oh in this conversation, okay. I'll stay.

00:25:54.780 --> 00:25:56.920
*laughter*

00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:58.760
Dr. Dagny: Thanks Ali. I was gonna be offended

00:25:58.760 --> 00:25:59.960
Dr. Cedric: Yeah.

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:01.670
I was like dang, dude!

00:26:01.670 --> 00:26:06.710
Alex: I'm also an open-minded person and I understand the frustration from both sides is a nuance.

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:09.110
It's not a one or the other issue technically

00:26:09.110 --> 00:26:13.780
I think that there obviously is compromise that should and can be made.

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:18.300
Julia: Dagny, you said something that I thought was pretty profound,

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:22.360
- being pro isolation doesn't mean anti economy, you know,

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:26.040
I'm guilty of looking at the other side and thinking

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:27.480
You people just don't even care.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:32.300
You've said yourself you're having you know, your personal situation that has been impacted.

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:35.960
And so, so I really appreciated that point that you made

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:38.560
and then getting a better understanding of where you're coming from.

00:26:38.560 --> 00:26:41.420
Dr. Cedric: This is one of the most pleasant conversations

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:48.400
I have ever had in terms of being able to discuss politics, science, current events,

00:26:48.400 --> 00:26:55.200
in a way that was productive, and so with that you learn a lot from everyone who is included.

00:26:55.200 --> 00:27:01.360
What it shows to me is it shows the model behavior that I feel like our country should have

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:09.000
in regards to how to be productive about setting appropriate restrictions,

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:14.200
setting appropriate business opportunities for everyone and I think it can be done.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:19.220
I've learned so much from you guys like I wrote it all down actually and I'm gonna take it with me,

00:27:19.220 --> 00:27:22.500
put it here and use it next week.

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:23.960
Julia: I feel like we're friends. I enjoyed it.

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:28.480
Dr. Cedric: Austin, Dags, once they lift these restrictions, we're all getting together.

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:29.280
Austin: Let's do it.

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:30.780
Alex: I live in Orange County too actually.

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:32.440
Dr. Cedric: Alex, Ally, and Julia.

